Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
you know ever since we started hearing  about the labor shortage situation here  in the u.s there's been so much blame to  go around some people like to blame lazy  millennials other people like to blame  the joe biden administration other  people like to blame poor people who are  just unwilling to go get a job and you  know there's been this idea that because  of the boosted unemployment benefits  that meant that many people were just  one willing to get a job they wanted to  just stay home and live off of  government resources the same was said  about the stimulus checks with the idea  that okay well if you get a thousand  dollars here and there you don't really  need to work which is kind of silly  really because obviously a thousand  bucks or 1200 bucks fourteen hundred  dollars that's not going to be enough to  cover your long-term basic needs and  indeed the labor shortage does persist  the number of job openings was at an  all-time high as of october at more than  11 million openings and companies are  struggling still to hire enough workers  to meet the demand but the truth is that  the labor shortage is caused by a  multitude of factors and chief among  them have to do with baby boomers  retiring early and a reduction in worker  visas that have been granted over the  course of the pandemic overall i was  able to come up with eight reasons in  total for the labor shortage situation  so i wanted to run through them all with  you here so number one and these are in  no particular order people are retiring  early estimates suggest that the us has  about two million more retirees right  now than we otherwise would have  expected if we had not had the pandemic  and this makes sense because let's say  you were 62 years old when the pandemic  started and you planned on retiring at  age 65 well by now you're about 63 and a  half going on 64. do you really want to  go back to work for another year or so  especially when you already are  semi-retired as it is probably not  especially given the fact that the stock  market has boomed over the past couple  of years so people's retirement accounts  are higher than they expected them to be  also real estate values have gone up  significantly so people assuming they  own their house and assuming they have a  401k or an ira uh they've seen a  sizeable growth in their wealth over the  past couple of years making it so they  may not have to keep working they might  as well just retire early number two has  to do with immigration the u.s  government has issued about 1.2 million  fewer visas to adult immigrants refugees  and temporary foreign workers and i  think this is intuitive given that  restrictions on travel were in existence  especially during the early phase of the  pandemic many of these restrictions  still exist today there's a chance also  that uh you know things can turn around  because now the department of homeland  security has announced that the biden  administration is going to be adding 20  000 new slots to a seasonal guest worker  program for the winter season but it  makes sense that given there's a  pandemic raging throughout the world  people just don't want to be traveling  from country to country number three  there's a skills mismatch 48 of workers  said that they were frustrated with the  search and 46 said that they can only  find low paying rules just because a job  is available that doesn't mean that  people necessarily want to take that job  it doesn't necessarily mean that it's a  good paying job and we know that some  people were able to take advantage of  their time off during the pandemic and  many people learned new skills they did  online classes etc and so their market  value has improved but if they don't  find a job that matches their skill  improvement maybe they're just going to  decide to hold out  number four commuting uh people many  times just don't want to go back to  commuting obviously during the beginning  phase of the pandemic many workers moved  out of the areas that would be hiring by  now like they moved from cities to rural  areas and they just have decided they  don't want to commute anymore that's  been my experience like i like working  from home it wouldn't be a total deal  breaker for me if i had to start going  back into an office every day but i'd  prefer to just keep working from home  obviously commuting can be very very  stressful especially if you have a long  commute like if you're driving an hour  to and from work every day i mean that's  just crazy and if you don't have to do  it anymore why would you want to so  commuting obviously is a big factor  number five child care  most schools are open at this point but  there can be cancellations based on  coveted cases at the drop of a hat maybe  the school year gets halted early  obviously we're seeing some closures now  with omicron  so sometimes parents who may be working  part-time while the kids are at school  they can't do that anymore because they  have to be home to take care of the  child or at least they have to keep that  as an option in case school does get  shut down number six is protests over  vaccine mandates there are some people  who are just not going to get the  vaccine no matter what it doesn't matter  if you offer them a carrot or a stick  and so obviously if they refuse to get  the vaccine there are so many jobs that  they're no longer eligible for they  can't work for a government agency or  they can't be a government contractor  they can't work for a company with  more than 50 employees they can't work  in certain cities in the country so  obviously that lowers their  possibilities when it comes to the job  market and that's also contributing in  part to the labor shortage number seven  fear of the virus cases are obviously  surging out of control right now the  omicron variant is super contagious and  so there are people who are maybe older  or immuno suppressed and they feel as  though you know i want to get a job but  i don't want to put my life at risk by  doing so and number eight the great  resignation this is a term coined by  organizational psychologist anthony  klotz who theorizes that people  especially pre-pandemic they would tie  in their identity with their work and  now with people taking some time off and  really being confronted with their  mortality because the amount of people  who have died over the past couple of  years it's caused a shift in their  perspective and they no longer wrap up  their identity with what they do as a  job and i think that you know maybe the  great resignation talking point is a  little bit overblown because after all  people need to work to survive most  people do anyway  but you know there could be some people  who decide i'm not going to work 40 to  50 hours a week anymore i'm going to  work part time or maybe i'll take a few  more years off and resume working later  maybe i want to  decide that i'm going to do something  else with my life so it's definitely a  mix of factors some of these things  you're going to be able to improve over  time other things you may not be able to  like for example the baby boomers  retiring early are you really going to  be able to bring them back into the job  market probably not they're probably  retired and not going to be entering the  job force again at least not in mass but  then there's other factors that will  improve as the state of coronavirus in  this country improves as we finally see  cases go down we finally start to see  deaths go down whenever that may be the  economy will follow we've been saying  this for two years now that you have to  deal with the virus before you can deal  with the economy and we'll hope that  this all sort of sorts itself out but  that's the sort of update now having to  do with the labor shortage and many of  the reasons why it's happening

